<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sd1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SD1: Matrix Essentials</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use matrix algebra to compute linear combinations, products of matrices, and determinants of matrices, as well as determine when these are not possible.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-matrices">
        <title>Matrices</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-matrix">
            <title>Matrix</title>
            <idx><h>matrix (definition)</h></idx>
            <idx><h>matrix (square)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>matrix</term> is an array of the form 
                    <md>
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
                            a_{21} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
                            \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \cdots \\
                            a_{m1} \amp a_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp a_{mn} \\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    where <m>m</m> represents the number of <em>rows</em> in the matrix and <m>n</m> represents the number of <em>columns</em> in the matrix. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    If <m>m = n</m>, i.e. the number of rows and columns are the same, then we say matrix is a <term>square matrix</term>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An example of a <m>3\times 4</m> matrix is given by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 7 \amp \dfrac{1}{7} \amp e \amp \pi \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-matrix-algebra">
        <title>Matrix Algebra</title>
        
        <assemblage>
            <title>Adding/Subtracting Matrices</title>
            <p>
                Let
                <md>
                        A = 
                        \begin{pmatrix} 
                            a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
                            a_{21} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
                            \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                            a_{m1} \amp a_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp a_{mn}\\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                and 
                <md>
                        B = 
                        \begin{pmatrix} 
                            b_{11} \amp b_{12} \amp \cdots \amp b_{1n} \\
                            b_{21} \amp b_{22} \amp \cdots \amp b_{2n} \\
                            \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                            b_{m1} \amp b_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp b_{mn}\\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                be two <m>m\times n</m> matrices. We define their sum/difference to be
                <md>
                    A \pm B = 
                    \begin{pmatrix}
                        a_{11} \pm b_{11} \amp a_{12}\pm b_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n}\pm b_{1n}\\
                        a_{21} \pm b_{21} \amp a_{22}\pm b_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n}\pm b_{2n}\\
                        \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                        a_{m1} \pm b_{m1} \amp a_{m2} \mp b_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp a_{mn} \pm b_{mn}\\
                    \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Note</alert>: In order to add/subtract matrices, they must have the row dimension and the same column dimension.
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If 
                    <md>
                        A = \begin{pmatrix}
                            2 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
                            0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
                            -8 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        B = \begin{pmatrix}
                            6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                            0 \amp 5 \amp 3 \\
                            13 \amp 7 \amp 9\\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    then
                    <md>
                        A + B =
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            2 + 6 \amp 3 + 0 \amp -1 + 1 \\
                            0 + 0 \amp -1 + 5 \amp 1 + 3 \\
                            -8 + 13 \amp 0 + 7 \amp 0 + 9\\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                        = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            8 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                            0 \amp 4 \amp 4 \\
                            5 \amp 7 \amp 9
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    while
                    <md>
                        A - B =
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            2 - 6 \amp 3 - 0 \amp -1 - 1 \\
                            0 - 0 \amp -1 - 5 \amp 1 - 3 \\
                            -8 - 13 \amp 0 - 7 \amp 0 - 9\\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                        = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            -4 \amp 3 \amp -2 \\
                            0 \amp -6 \amp -2 \\
                            -21 \amp -7 \amp -9 \\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Matrix Multiplication</title>
            <p>
                Let
                <md>
                    A = \begin{pmatrix}
                        a_{11} \amp a_{21} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
                        a_{21} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
                        \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                        a_{m1} \amp a_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp a_{mn}
                    \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and 
                <md>
                    B = \begin{pmatrix}
                        b_{11} \amp b_{21} \amp \cdots \amp b_{1k} \\
                        b_{21} \amp b_{22} \amp \cdots \amp b_{2k} \\
                        \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                        b_{n1} \amp b_{k2} \amp \cdots \amp b_{nk}
                    \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                be an <m>n\times k</m> matrix. We define their product to be the <m>m\times k</m> matrix given by
                <md>
                    AB = 
                    \begin{pmatrix}
                        \sum_{j=1}^n a_{1j}b_{j1} \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{1j}b_{j2} \amp \cdots \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{1j}b_{jk} \\
                        \sum_{j=1}^n a_{2j}b_{j1} \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{2j}b_{j2} \amp \cdots \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{2j}b_{jk} \\
                        \vdots \amp \cdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                        \sum_{j=1}^n a_{mj}b_{j1} \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{mj}b_{j2} \amp \cdots \amp \sum_{j=1}^n a_{mj}b_{jk}
                    \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <note>
            <p>
                A few notes about the matrix product:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            It is, in general, not commutative. That is, <m>AB \neq BA</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The matrix product is essentially the dot product between rows of <m>A</m> and columns of <m>B</m>. This is why the columns of <m>A</m> have to match the rows of <m>B</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </note>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If 
                    <md>
                        A = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            1 \amp 2 \\
                            3 \amp 4 \\
                            0 \amp -1 \\
                            10 \amp 0
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    (which is a <m>4\times 2</m> matrix) and
                    <md>
                        B = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
                            9 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp -2
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    (which is a <m>2\times 4</m> matrix), then their product is the <m>4\times 4</m> matrix
                    <md>
                        AB = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            18 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                            36 \amp -1 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
                            -9 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                            0 \amp 10 \amp 20 \amp 30 \\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    while the product <m>BA</m> does not exist because the columns of <m>B</m> are not the same dimension as the rows of <m>A</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let
                    <md>
                        A = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            7 \amp 1 \\
                            -2 \amp 2
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        B =
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            0 \amp 1 \\
                            -1 \amp 1
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    Then
                    <md>
                        AB = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            -1 \amp 8 \\
                            -2 \amp 0
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    Since <m>A</m> is a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix and <m>B</m> is a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix, we can compute <m>BA</m> as well:
                    <md>
                        BA = 
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            -2 \amp 2 \\
                            -9 \amp 1
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-matrix-determinants">
        <title>Determinants</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-determinant">
            <title>Determinant</title>
            <idx><h>determinant</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>determinant</term> of a matrix is a measurement of how the matrix transforms area/volume in space. The determinant is only possible with <m>n\times n</m> (square) matrices.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The determinant of a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{pmatrix}</m> is given by
                    <md>
                        \det(A) = \left|\begin{matrix}a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{matrix} \right| = ad - bc
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The determinant of a <m>3\times 3</m> matrix <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}a \amp b \amp c \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ g \amp h \amp i \end{pmatrix}</m> is given by
                    <md>
                        \det(A) = \left|\begin{matrix}a \amp b \amp c \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ g \amp h \amp i \end{matrix}\right| = a(ei-fh) - b(di-gf) + c(dh-ge)
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 6\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then,
                    <md>
                        \det(A) = \left|\begin{matrix} 4 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 6\end{matrix}\right| = 24 - (-2) = 26
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        \det(B) = \left|
                        \begin{matrix} 
                            1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 
                            -2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 
                            0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
                        \end{matrix}
                        \right| 
                        = 1(1-3) - 0(-2-0) + 1(-2-0) = -4
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>
</section>